{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And you shall make the altar.</b> The notarikon: מחילה (forgiveness), זכות (merit), ברכה (blessing), חיים (life)." "<b>Five cubits.</b> Its length corresponds to the tablets — five commandments on this tablet and five on that tablet. And three cubits its height corresponds to three redeemers who stood for Israel: Moses, Aaron, and Miriam. Some say: three cubits is its height from the edge of the *sovav* and upward, and that *sovav* is the *karkov* that is above the *reshet*. And the *reshet* is like a garment for the altar, made hole upon hole in the manner of a netting, and it is for ornament. It begins from the ground and goes upward to half the altar, five cubits. And immediately the *karkov* begins and goes upward from it by one cubit, and it is called *sovav*. And from that *sovav* and upward was three cubits up to the horns, according to the one who says: three cubits from the edge of the *sovav* and upward. And the horns — their height is one cubit. Therefore, in total: ten cubits of height. And also the horns have one cubit square, both width and height. The altar was five cubits long and five cubits wide. And the horns, whose thickness was one cubit, would reduce one cubit on each side, and if so, there was between horn and horn only three cubits. And inside the horns was one cubit for the walking of the priests' feet on each side, and one cubit by one cubit remained in between. And that one cubit by one cubit was the place of the arrangement [of the wood and offerings]. And even though no ramp is explicitly stated in the verse, even so, it is established for us that it had a ramp, for it is written: לא תעלה במעלות. And it was on the south side of the altar, but on any other side it could not be, as the Talmud demonstrates."], :book :exodus, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:04:11.531619819Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}